Installing HemeLB
=================

This file describes fix/hemelb-improvements. Use the guides in the branch
being built: solver XML versions and pressure units differ between branches.
The complete installation guide is doc/user/install.md.

Quick install: macOS or Debian/Ubuntu Linux
==========================================

    git clone --branch fix/hemelb-improvements https://github.com/lepotatoguy/hemelb.git
    cd hemelb
    Scripts/install_hemelb.sh

Defaults:
  Solver prefix: ~/.local/hemelb
  Tool environment: conda environment gmy-tool
  System packages: installed through Homebrew or apt unless skipped
  Tests: hemelb-tests is built and run

Useful options:
  --prefix DIR        Choose the solver installation prefix
  --env-name NAME     Choose a fresh conda environment name
  --env-prefix DIR    Choose the conda environment by path instead
  --jobs N            Control parallel build jobs
  --no-system-deps    Use system packages already installed
  --no-gmy-tool       Install only the solver, skipping both Python tools
  --no-tests          Skip executing unit tests; they are still built
  --no-gui            Skip macOS GUI launcher setup

After installation:

    export PATH="$HOME/.local/hemelb/bin:$PATH"
    conda activate gmy-tool

The quick-start guide is doc/user/getting-started.md. It uses the bundled
cylinder geometry and examples/first-run.xml, including field output
and checkpoints. A full geometry-tool installation is not needed to run
prepared XML/GMY inputs.

Installing HemeLB manually
=========================

    cmake -S . -B build -G "Unix Makefiles" \
          -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
          -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="$HOME/.local/hemelb" \
          -DHEMELB_SUBPROJECT_MAKE_JOBS=4
    cmake --build build -j 4

The top-level super build finds or builds dependencies, then builds and
installs the solver. It invokes make for the solver subproject. Use Code/
as the source for a code-only build with existing dependencies; use
dependencies/ for dependency preparation. See doc/user/main-application.md
and doc/user/CMakeOptions.md for layouts, library hints, and model options.

Requirements and version sources
================================

The solver requires a C++20 compiler, CMake >= 3.13, MPI >= 3.0, Boost
headers >= 1.77, ParMETIS 4.x, METIS, TinyXML 2.x, CTemplate, and zlib.
TinyXML2 is a different library and is not used by this branch. Tests use
Catch2 2.x. RBC builds additionally need HDF5 and VTK; multiscale builds
need MPWide.

The fallback versions are specified by dependency build files:
  dependencies/<Name>/build.cmake
  Boost 1.77.0; ParMETIS 4.0.2; TinyXML 2.6.2; CTemplate 2.4;
  zlib 1.2.6; Catch2 2.13.9; HDF5 1.8.15; VTK 9.1.0; MPWide 1.1.

The geometry tool and Python tools have their own dependency environment:
  geometry-tool/conda-environment.yml: main package pins
  geometry-tool/conda-lock/osx-64.txt: exact Intel macOS package URLs
  geometry-tool/conda-lock/linux-64.txt: exact Linux x86_64 package URLs
  geometry-tool/pyproject.toml: pip build-isolation pins

The install script uses the platform lock and installs both packages with
--no-deps --no-build-isolation. The geometry tool environment supports
Python 3.8 through 3.11; the Python-tools CI matrix covers 3.8 through 3.13.
On Apple Silicon the solver is native, while the geometry-tool environment
uses osx-64 under Rosetta. Keep those build environments separate.

System compiler, MPI, CMake, and package-manager versions are not pinned
by this repository. CI configuration lives in .github/workflows/; its
matrix describes intended coverage, not the outcome of a particular run.
Recorded earlier validation is in CHANGELOG.md. See doc/dev/README.md for
local tests and doc/user/troubleshooting.md for common failures.
